	(text "Serial polarity: the sign's RX (J2 pin 6, DB9 8) reads a space as 1 and has a pull-up to 5 V (3.9k on a CC784-3A), so U3A pulls it low for a mark: ESPHome tx_pin inverted: true."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 18.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "02ef7c46-4a52-4ecb-9603-a067da9d6f56")
	)
	(text "Power: J2 pin 1 (DB9 9) is +5 V through the sign's 10 ohm R31. F1, D1 and C2-C4 ride the Wi-Fi bursts; D2 lets USB power the dongle alone without back-feeding the sign."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 30.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "0ac98dfb-9989-4db1-b4c5-4b78dc34649c")
	)
	(text "Pin swap (2026-11-05): the first boards, from the October 2026 gerbers, have J2 pins 8 and 9 swapped (DB9_2 on pin 8, DB9_1 on pin 9), after an SK1 table that was later corrected by tracing the board. Fixed here: SK1 8 = DB9 1 (PORT_IN bit 1), SK1 9 = DB9 2 (PORT_IN bit 0). On those boards keep JP1 and JP2 open: JP1 would ground SK1 8."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 34.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "28641fa5-d3fc-4c5c-a9db-8afbc079119c")
	)
	(text "GPIO map (J2 pin, DB9 equivalent in brackets): IO5 TX -> 6 (8)   IO0 RX <- 3 (3)   IO1 <- 4 (4)   IO6 <- 5 (7)   IO3 -> 9 (2)   IO4 -> 7 (6)   IO2 -> 8 (1) (inverted by U9, then U4B, R5, JP2)   IO14 J2-8 source: low = IO2, high = U7 (RN1 pulls it high)   IO7 <- 8 (1) (U5B)   IO8 WS2812 LED   IO9 BOOT   IO12/13 USB"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 24.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "501b42fc-cc4d-4896-9485-c29882d5214c")
	)
	(text "The sign's TX (J2 pin 3) idles low; U6A inverts it, so rx_pin is not inverted. R1 keeps J2 pin 6 at mark until the ESP configures IO5."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 21.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "516c6344-beeb-46ad-9be0-e25432052d1d")
	)
	(text "CC784 2x5 IDC dongle: ESP32-C6 serial proxy for signs with the 10-pin keypad header (CC784-3A board: CC710, original CC784), powered from the sign. J2 carries the DB9 signals of the CC784-4 in the CC784-3A's SK1 order."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 14.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2 2)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "886a430f-e41c-49d2-9d02-3d8bec9f459e")
	)
	(text "With the patched firmware (patch 36 on), the sign reads its IR receiver on PORT_IN bit 4 (UPGRADES.md section 8), so J2 pin 8 (DB9 1) is keypad column bit 1, not an IR input: keep JP2 open. Stock firmware only: on a CC710, J2 pin 8 is keypad column bit 1, which must be high for the sign to transmit or receive, and held low at power-up in keypad mode it clears the message store, so keep JP2 open there (bridged, with U7 selected and idle, U4B holds pin 8 low). The stock CC710 enters packet mode only when its boot probe is answered (data J2-5 on IO6, clock J2-4 on IO1, answer J2-9 on IO3, a few microseconds per bit; firmware notes section 15), and JP1 grounding J2-9 selects its serial terminal mode instead (on a stock CC784NET, the PC detect)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20 27.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "bdcb4f71-74dc-4106-b5e0-5c6a9a98884b")
	)
